# Authorization

Wyrd authorizes every API request with its own RBAC permission check on the
verified principal, recorded to canonical audit. There is no separate
runtime policy plane or extension seam.

For how a caller obtains a token in the first place, see
[Authentication](/concepts/authentication/); for the whole identity picture, see
[Identity and auth](/concepts/identity-and-auth/).

## RBAC

A `Permission` is `{ resource, action, scope }`: `resource` and `action` name
the operation, and the typed `scope` names the objects the grant reaches. Scope
is either `all` (every object of that resource) or a domain-tagged object
identity — today `bifrost`, as a `{ catalog, schema }` schema scope or a
`{ catalog, schema, table_uid }` table scope. Scope is required on every stored
permission; a scope-less entry is rejected at decode, and there is no
compatibility decoder. A grant subsumes a request only when all three axes
cover it.

Roles carry a JSONB set of permissions. Issuance resolves the principal's roles
once and signs the result into the token's `permissions` claim; verification is
local and reads no database, so a grant change governs the next token and an
issued token keeps its snapshot for at most five minutes.

```json
[
  { "resource": "cards", "action": "read", "scope": "all" },
  {
    "resource": "bifrost_query",
    "action": "read",
    "scope": { "bifrost": { "schema": { "catalog": "vala", "schema": "logs" } } }
  }
]
```

Object scope stays inside RBAC. It is a static role grant over named objects,
not an attribute or context rule.

### Routes whose objects are not known until planning

A Bifrost SQL query does not name its tables until the statement is planned, so
the route admits on the coarse operation capability (does this principal hold
*any* `bifrost_query:read` grant?) and the authoritative decision runs inside
the Oracle against the fully resolved table set — before admission, read-audit
acceptance, peer dispatch, or any source read. One uncovered table denies the
whole query with `WYRD_VALA_403_QUERY_FORBIDDEN` and returns no rows. The
approved scoped decision is bound into the distributed permission digest, so a
peer worker cannot widen it.

The resolution path:
1. At issuance, the server reads the principal's current roles from the
   tenant's role table and fetches their permission sets.
2. It signs those merged permissions into the token's `permissions` claim; the
   `roles` claim is informational only.
3. On verify, `effective_permissions` is built from that claim with no database
   read. Merging applies, applying wildcard (`resource:*`) and
   `AnyOf` subsumption. A role with `cards:*` subsumes `cards:read`, `cards:write`,
   and every other `cards` action.

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Built-in role', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Permissions' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['admin', 'All resources and actions (*)'],
    ['writer', 'cards:read, cards:write, artifacts:read, artifacts:write, operators:invoke, evals:run, triggers:write'],
    ['reader', 'cards:read, artifacts:read, audit:read'],
    ['agent', 'cards:read, cards:write, artifacts:read, artifacts:write, operators:invoke, evals:run'],
    ['runtime_admin', 'service_accounts:write'],
    ['workload', 'bifrost_table:read, bifrost_record:write, bifrost_query:read'],
    ['wyrd_default', 'bifrost_table:read, bifrost_record:write, evals:run']
  ]}
/>

Built-in roles are seeded per tenant at tenant creation. A Service or Agent
Card's principal receives `wyrd_default` when its Card is first registered, so
the key `wyrd auth issue-key` issues for it can start Bifrost, emit its
evidence, and run its Verifiers without a manual grant. Its Card scope still
bounds which Cards it may emit for and verify. Re-registering the Card never
re-grants a revoked `wyrd_default` role.

`wyrd_default` does not read the tenant's Bifrost tables. A Service that
queries them needs the `workload` role, granted by a tenant administrator:

```bash
wyrd auth grant-role --kind Service --name my-svc --version 1.0.0 \
  --space prod --role workload
```

The command calls `POST /v1/auth/grant-role` with `{ card_ref, role }` and
prints the principal's roles. It requires tenant administration (`*`), so
`runtime_admin` cannot use it. It names the target by its Card, so a human
user is never a target; their roles come from federated login. A grant
already held succeeds with `granted: false`. An unknown or foreign Card
answers `WYRD_AUTH_404_PRINCIPAL_NOT_FOUND`, and an unknown role
`WYRD_SPEC_400_VALIDATION`. The new role reaches the principal's tokens at its
next key exchange. There is no revoke command.

The `operators` resource has three actions. `operators:invoke` lets a
principal register a verification binding that names an `on_failure` Operator
(alongside `cards:write`). `operators:read` lists and reads redacted
[Operator connections](/how-to/operate/operator-connections/), and
`operators:write` creates, rotates, updates, disables, and re-enables them. No
connection operation is authorized by `operators:invoke` alone. Of the
built-in roles, only `admin` holds `operators:read` and `operators:write`;
grant them to another role explicitly.

## Two "kind" concepts

Two distinct "kind" fields appear in auth that are easy to confuse:

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Field', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Type' },
    { header: 'What it means' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['PrincipalKind', 'TenantAdmin | User | Service { card_ref: Option<CardRef> } | Agent { card_ref }', 'The identity of the tenant-plane token holder: who is acting. A Service card binding is optional; an Agent is always card-bound. Platform-plane principals carry PlatformPrincipal instead and never reach a tenant route.'],
    ['PrincipalKindPolicy', 'Human | Workload', 'Per-issuer config: whether an OIDC issuer is for human login or machine workloads.']
  ]}
/>

`PrincipalKind` is encoded in the token and resolved on every verify.
`PrincipalKindPolicy` is checked once during token issuance to ensure a
workload assertion is not used for human login and vice versa.

## Delegation

Service-to-service calls run under a **delegated token** (RFC 8693). When
Service B handles a request for Service A, B exchanges A's token for one that
acts for A: it calls `POST /auth/token` with the `token-exchange` grant, A's
token as `subject_token`, its own token as `actor_token`, and a target
`audience`. The issued token names A as its principal and B as its outer `act`,
and carries only the permissions both A and B hold, so B can never do more
than A could. The `act` chain is attribution for audit; it confers no
authority, and exchange consults no policy. The token has no refresh token and
delegation depth is capped at 5.

A token issued for the `bifrost` audience is accepted only on the Bifrost ingest
and query surfaces, so it cannot be replayed against the rest of Wyrd.

<WyrdSequenceDiagram
  title="Delegation (token-exchange)"
  participants={['Service B', 'Wyrd server']}
  messages={[
    { from: 'Service B', to: 'Wyrd server', label: 'POST /auth/token · token-exchange · subject_token=A · actor_token=B · audience' },
    { from: 'Wyrd server', to: 'Wyrd server', label: 'verify both tokens · same tenant · actor is a card-bound Service/Agent' },
    { from: 'Wyrd server', to: 'Wyrd server', label: 'permissions = B ∩ A · audit subject A, actor B' },
    { from: 'Wyrd server', to: 'Service B', label: 'token · sub=A · act.sub=B · aud · no refresh', dashed: true }
  ]}
/>

The SDKs perform the exchange for you and re-exchange before expiry:
`client.on_behalf_of(a_token, TokenAudience::Bifrost)` in Rust,
`client.on_behalf_of(a_token, audience="bifrost")` in Python, and
`client.onBehalfOf(aToken, { audience: "bifrost" })` in TypeScript.

